WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.377 --> 00:00:02.517
- [Voiceover] There's three things

00:00:02.517 --> 00:00:04.328
that are most important to chefs.

00:00:04.328 --> 00:00:05.628
First is flavor,

00:00:05.628 --> 00:00:07.068
second is flavor,

00:00:07.068 --> 00:00:08.670
and third is flavor.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:09.753
Flavor rules.

00:00:11.642 --> 00:00:13.175
(tractor putting)

00:00:13.175 --> 00:00:15.148
- [Voiceover] Built on the
fertile former-lake-bottom soil

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:17.575
of northern Ohio, The
Chef's Garden is a 300-acre

00:00:17.575 --> 00:00:20.083
family farm that grows specialty produce

00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:22.265
for some of the world's best restaurants.

00:00:22.265 --> 00:00:25.052
- The Chef's Garden is a small family farm

00:00:25.052 --> 00:00:27.118
that has the good fortune
of working directly

00:00:27.118 --> 00:00:29.255
with some of the greatest
chefs in the world.

00:00:29.255 --> 00:00:31.402
In many cases we're
working with plant growers

00:00:31.402 --> 00:00:35.640
that are breeding to be
able to create new plants,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.241
not genetic modification
but genetic selection.

00:00:39.241 --> 00:00:40.342
- [Voiceover] The farm grows produce

00:00:40.342 --> 00:00:41.860
of all shapes and sizes,

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.427
like miniature carrots,

00:00:43.427 --> 00:00:44.855
these white strawberries,

00:00:44.855 --> 00:00:47.781
and almost 100 varieties of microgreens.

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:51.948
But the Jones family wasn't
always The Chef's Garden.

00:00:53.447 --> 00:00:56.906
- My parents had had some
very successful years.

00:00:56.906 --> 00:00:59.879
In 1983 they had a very
devastating hailstorm

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:02.712
and ultimately the farm collapsed.

00:01:04.824 --> 00:01:06.299
We were dealt a new deck of cards

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:09.632
and found opportunity within a disaster.

00:01:13.137 --> 00:01:16.179
We met a European-influenced
Chef, Iris Baylen.

00:01:16.179 --> 00:01:18.524
Said: "Grow me varieties for flavor.

00:01:18.524 --> 00:01:21.195
"Grow me varieties without chemical.

00:01:21.195 --> 00:01:22.924
"I want a zucchini bloom."

00:01:22.924 --> 00:01:24.945
Thought this lady was absolutely crazy.

00:01:24.945 --> 00:01:26.593
Little did we know, she
knew a heck of a lot

00:01:26.593 --> 00:01:28.079
more about it than we did.

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:29.519
And that really opened our eyes

00:01:29.519 --> 00:01:32.038
to another whole world out there.

00:01:32.038 --> 00:01:34.705
We hooked up with some
really great chefs early on,

00:01:34.705 --> 00:01:37.898
folks like Danielle Balou
and John George Von Richten

00:01:37.898 --> 00:01:40.406
and Alain Ducasse and Michel Richard,

00:01:40.406 --> 00:01:42.461
and then Thomas Keller and Charlie Trotter

00:01:42.461 --> 00:01:45.259
and Ritz Carlton chefs
and Four Season chefs.

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:47.790
And they've allowed us an
existence in agriculture.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.634
We're indebted to those
chefs that have given us

00:01:50.634 --> 00:01:52.480
the privilege and the path to be able

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.063
to follow our dream of farming.

00:01:58.204 --> 00:01:59.806
So it's that symbiotic relationship

00:01:59.806 --> 00:02:02.732
of chef and farmer working
together for the greater good.

00:02:02.732 --> 00:02:06.935
And here we are 35 years later
still working to get better.

00:02:06.935 --> 00:02:11.265
- To the best of our ability,
right now, in this place,

00:02:11.265 --> 00:02:13.111
I think we're doing the right thing,

00:02:13.111 --> 00:02:15.410
and I think that trumps every other reason

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.175
for whatever you do in this life.

00:02:17.175 --> 00:02:20.925
(soft acoustic guitar music)

